  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value; steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first. Starting point (0.6, 1.7). Above a learning rate of 2/3 the x swings grow and the run diverges.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with size 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly zero at 64.",
    "Arrows use scale 1: each is as long as its step in bowl units. Orange is the actual mini-batch step; grey is −learning rate × true slope. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn square (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step; positions off the bowl are capped on screen.",
    "With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average; small batches can raise it on single steps. The run advances 5 steps per second at normal speed."
  ]
}